// Chapter V: Advice from a Caterpillar — Complete (Pages 59–75)
// Includes Caterpillar header (#15 p. 59), 4 consecutive Father William plates + verse
// (#16 p. 63, #18 p. 64, #17 p. 65, #19 p. 66), and 5-4-5 asterism (p. 69).

\use "components/Align.dt"
\use "components/Spacer.dt"
\use "components/Italic.dt"
\use "AliceStyle.dt"

\Component ChapterFive(
    x: Distance = parent.left,
    y: Distance = parent.top,
    width: Distance = parent.width,
    pagination: Pagination = \Pagination(breakable: true),
    env font: Font,
    env chapter_meta: PageValue
) {
    // 19 * 17.1pt = 324.9pt leaves exact 3 opening lines on Page 59
    \let ch = \AliceChapterWithImage(
        x: x,
        y: y,
        width: width,
        number: "V",
        title: "ADVICE FROM A CATERPILLAR",
        verso_header: "ADVICE FROM A",
        recto_header: "CATERPILLAR.",
        img_src: "images/tenniel_15.png",
        header_height: 342.0pt,
        img_top: -10pt,
        ch_gap: 16pt,
        sub_gap: 16pt,
        break_before: Break.Page
    ) {
        // ============================================================
        // Pages 59–62: Dialogue with the Caterpillar
        // ============================================================
        \FlushPara {
            \DropInitial{T}//
            \SmallCaps{HE} Caterpillar and Alice looked at each other for some
            time in silence: at last the Caterpillar took the hookah out of its
            mouth, and addressed her in a languid, sleepy voice.
        }

        \Para {
            “Who are \Italic{you?}” said the Caterpillar.
        }

        \Para {
            This was not an encouraging opening for a conversation. Alice
            replied, rather shyly, “I—I hardly know, sir, just at present—at
            least I know who I \Italic{was} when I got up this morning, but I
            think I must have been changed several times since then.”
        }

        \Para {
            “What do you mean by that?” said the Caterpillar sternly. “Explain
            yourself!”
        }

        \Para {
            “I can’t explain \Italic{myself,} I’m afraid, sir,” said Alice,
            “because I’m not myself, you see.”
        }

        \Para {
            “I don’t see,” said the Caterpillar.
        }

        \Para {
            “I’m afraid I can’t put it more clearly,” Alice replied very
            politely, “for I can’t under\-stand it myself to begin with; and being
            so many different sizes in a day is very confusing.”
        }

        \Para {
            “It isn’t,” said the Caterpillar.
        }

        \Para {
            “Well, perhaps you haven’t found it so yet,” said Alice; “but when
            you have to turn into a chrysalis—you will some day, you know—and
            then after that into a butterfly, I should think you’ll feel it a
            little queer, won’t you?”
        }

        \Para {
            “Not a bit,” said the Caterpillar.
        }

        \Para {
            “Well, perhaps your feelings may be differ\-ent,” said Alice; “all I
            know is, it would feel very queer to \Italic{me.}”
        }

        \Para {
            “You!” said the Caterpillar contemptuously. “Who are \Italic{you?}”
        }

        \Para {
            Which brought them back again to the be\-ginning of the conversation.
            Alice felt a little irritated at the Caterpillar’s making such
            \Italic{very} short remarks, and she drew herself up and said, very
            gravely, “I think, you ought to tell me who \Italic{you} are, first.”
        }

        \Para {
            “Why?” said the Caterpillar.
        }

        \Para {
            Here was another puzzling question; and as Alice could not think of
            any good reason, and as the Caterpillar seemed to be in a
            \Italic{very} un\-pleasant state of mind, she turned away.
        }

        \Para {
            “Come back!” the Caterpillar called after her. “I’ve something
            important to say!”
        }

        \Para {
            This sounded promising, certainly: Alice turned and came back again.
        }

        \Para {
            “Keep your temper,” said the Caterpillar.
        }

        \Para {
            “Is that all?” said Alice, swallowing down her anger as well as she
            could.
        }

        \Para {
            “No,” said the Caterpillar.
        }

        \Para {
            Alice thought she might as well wait, as she had nothing else to do,
            and perhaps after all it might tell her something worth hearing. For
            some minutes it puffed away without speak\-ing, but at last it
            unfolded its arms, took the hookah out of its mouth again, and said,
            “So you think you’re changed, do you?”
        }

        \Para {
            “I’m afraid I am, sir,” said Alice; “I can’t remember things as I
            used—and I don’t keep the same size for ten minutes together!”
        }

        \Para {
            “Can’t remember \Italic{what} things?” said the Caterpillar.
        }

        \Para {
            “Well, I’ve tried to say ‘How doth the little busy bee,’ but it all
            came different!” Alice replied in a very melancholy voice.
        }

        \Para {
            “Repeat, ‘\Italic{You are old, Father William,}’” said the
            Caterpillar.
        }

        \Para {
            Alice folded her hands, and began:—
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Page 63 (Recto): Father William Plate 1 (Standing on his head)
        // ============================================================
        \TopPlate(src: "images/tenniel_16.png", grid_lines: 12.5, img_top: 4pt)
        \PoemBlock(size: 10pt, leading: 16.0pt, italic: true) {
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { “You are old, Father William,” the young man said, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 12pt) { “And your hair has become very white; }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { And yet you incessantly stand on your head— }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 12pt) { Do you think, at your age, it is right?” }
            \Spacer(height: 28pt)
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { “In my youth,” Father William replied to his son, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 12pt) { “I feared it might injure the brain; }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { But, now that I’m perfectly sure I have none, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 12pt) { Why, I do it again and again.” }
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Page 64 (Verso): Father William Plate 2 (Back-somersault)
        // ============================================================
        \TopPlate(src: "images/tenniel_18.png", grid_lines: 12.5, img_top: 5pt)
        \PoemBlock(size: 10pt, leading: 16.0pt, italic: true) {
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { “You are old,” said the youth, “as I mentioned before, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 12pt) { And have grown most uncommonly fat; }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { Yet you turned a back\=somersault in at the door— }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 12pt) { Pray, what is the reason of that?” }
            \Spacer(height: 28pt)
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { “In my youth,” said the sage, as he shook his grey locks, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 12pt) { “I kept all my limbs very supple }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { By the use of this ointment—one shilling the box— }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 12pt) { Allow me to sell you a couple?” }
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Page 65 (Recto, Signature F): Father William Plate 3 (The goose)
        // ============================================================
        \TopPlate(src: "images/tenniel_17.png", grid_lines: 12.5, img_top: 5pt)
        \PoemBlock(size: 10pt, leading: 16.0pt, italic: true) {
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { “You are old,” said the youth, “and your jaws are too weak }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 12pt) { For anything tougher than suet; }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { Yet you finished the goose, with the bones and the beak— }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 12pt) { Pray, how did you manage to do it?” }
            \Spacer(height: 28pt)
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { “In my youth,” said his father, “I took to the law, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 12pt) { And argued each case with my wife; }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { And the muscular strength, which it gave to my jaw, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 12pt) { Has lasted the rest of my life.” }
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Page 66 (Verso): Father William Plate 4 (Balancing the eel)
        // ============================================================
        \TopPlate(src: "images/tenniel_19.png", grid_lines: 12.5, img_top: 4pt)
        \PoemBlock(size: 10pt, leading: 16.0pt, italic: true) {
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { “You are old,” said the youth, “one would hardly suppose }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 12pt) { That your eye was as steady as ever; }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { Yet you balanced an eel on the end of your nose— }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 12pt) { What made you so awfully clever?” }
            \Spacer(height: 28pt)
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { “I have answered three questions, and that is enough,” }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 12pt) { Said his father; “don’t give yourself airs! }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { Do you think I can listen all day to such stuff? }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 12pt) { Be off, or I’ll kick you down stairs!” }
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Pages 67–69: Mushroom instructions & 5-4-5 Asterism
        // ============================================================
        \Para(pagination: \Pagination(breakable: true, break_before: Break.Page)) {
            “That is not said right,” said the Caterpillar.
        }

        \Para {
            “Not \Italic{quite} right, I’m afraid,” said Alice, timidly; “some
            of the words have got altered.”
        }

        \Para {
            “It is wrong from beginning to end,” said the Caterpillar decidedly,
            and there was silence for some minutes.
        }

        \Para {
            The Caterpillar was the first to speak.
        }

        \Para {
            “What size do you want to be?” it asked.
        }

        \Para {
            “Oh, I’m not particular as to size,” Alice hastily replied; “only
            one doesn’t like changing so often, you know.”
        }

        \Para {
            “I \Italic{don’t} know,” said the Caterpillar.
        }

        \Para {
            Alice said nothing: she had never been so much contradicted in her
            life before, and she felt that she was losing her temper.
        }

        \Para {
            “Are you content now?” said the Caterpillar.
        }

        \Para {
            “Well, I should like to be a \Italic{little} larger, sir, if you
            wouldn’t mind,” said Alice: “three inches is such a wretched height
            to be.”
        }

        \Para {
            “It is a very good height indeed!” said the Caterpillar angrily,
            rearing itself upright as it spoke (it was exactly three inches
            high).
        }

        \Para {
            “But I’m not used to it!” pleaded poor Alice in a piteous tone. And
            she thought to herself, “I wish the creatures wouldn’t be so easily
            offended!”
        }

        \Para {
            “You’ll get used to it in time,” said the Caterpillar; and it put
            the hookah into its mouth and began smoking again.
        }

        \Para {
            This time Alice waited patiently until it chose to speak again. In a
            minute or two the Caterpillar took the hookah out of its mouth and
            yawned once or twice, and shook itself. Then it got down off the
            mushroom, and crawled away in the grass, merely remarking as it
            went, “One side will make you grow taller, and the other side will
            make you grow shorter.”
        }

        \Para {
            “One side of \Italic{what?} The other side of \Italic{what?}”
            thought Alice to herself.
        }

        \Para {
            “Of the mushroom,” said the Caterpillar, just as if she had asked it
            aloud; and in another moment it was out of sight.
        }

        \Para {
            Alice remained looking thoughtfully at the mushroom for a minute,
            trying to make out which were the two sides of it; and as it was
            perfectly round, she found this a very difficult question. However,
            at last she stretched her arms round it as far as they would go, and
            broke off a bit of the edge with each hand.
        }

        \Para {
            “And now which is which?” she said to her\-self, and nibbled a little
            of the right\=hand bit to try the effect: the next moment she felt a
            vio\-lent blow underneath her chin: it had struck her foot!
        }

        \Para {
            She was a good deal frightened by this very sudden change, but she
            felt that there was no time to be lost, as she was shrinking
            rapidly; so she set to work at once to eat some of the other bit.
            Her chin was pressed so closely against her foot, that there was
            hardly room to open her mouth; but she did it at last, and managed
            to swallow a morsel of the left\=hand bit.
        }

        \Asterism545()

        // ============================================================
        // Pages 70–75: The Pigeon & returning to normal height
        // ============================================================
        \Para(pagination: \Pagination(breakable: true, break_before: Break.Page)) {
            “Come, my head’s free at last!” said Alice in a tone of delight,
            which changed into alarm in another moment, when she found that her
            shoulders were nowhere to be found: all she could see, when she
            looked down, was an im\-mense length of neck, which seemed to rise
            like a stalk out of a sea of green leaves that lay far below her.
        }

        \Para {
            “What \Italic{can} all that green stuff be?” said Alice. “And where
            \Italic{have} my shoulders got to? And oh, my poor hands, how is it
            I can’t see you?” She was moving them about as she spoke, but no
            result seemed to follow, except a little shaking among the distant
            green leaves.
        }

        \Para {
            As there seemed to be no chance of getting her hands up to her head,
            she tried to get her head down to them, and was delighted to find
            that her neck would bend about easily in any direction, like a
            serpent. She had just succeeded in curving it down into a graceful
            zigzag, and was going to dive in among the leaves, which she found
            to be nothing but the tops of the trees under which she had been
            wandering, when a sharp hiss made her draw back in a hurry: a large
            pigeon had flown into her face, and was beating her violently with
            its wings.
        }

        \Para {
            “Serpent!” screamed the Pigeon.
        }

        \Para {
            “I’m \Italic{not} a serpent!” said Alice indignantly. “Let me alone!”
        }

        \Para {
            “Serpent, I say again!” repeated the Pigeon, but in a more subdued
            tone, and added with a kind of sob, “I’ve tried every way, and
            nothing seems to suit them!”
        }

        \Para {
            “I haven’t the least idea what you’re talk\-ing about,” said Alice.
        }

        \Para {
            “I’ve tried the roots of trees, and I’ve tried banks, and I’ve tried
            hedges,” the Pigeon went on, without attending to her; “but those
            serpents! There’s no pleasing them!”
        }

        \Para {
            Alice was more and more puzzled, but she thought there was no use in
            saying anything more till the Pigeon had finished.
        }

        \Para {
            “As if it wasn’t trouble enough hatching the eggs,” said the Pigeon;
            “but I must be on the look\=out for serpents night and day! Why, I
            haven’t had a wink of sleep these three weeks!”
        }

        \Para {
            “I’m very sorry you’ve been annoyed,” said Alice, who was beginning
            to see its meaning.
        }

        \Para {
            “And just as I’d taken the highest tree in the wood,” continued the
            Pigeon, raising its voice to a shriek, “and just as I was thinking I
            should be free of them at last, they must needs come wriggling down
            from the sky! Ugh, Serpent!”
        }

        \Para {
            “But I’m \Italic{not} a serpent, I tell you!” said Alice. “I’m a——
            I’m a——”
        }

        \Para {
            “Well! \Italic{What} are you?” said the Pigeon. “I can see you’re
            trying to invent something!”
        }

        \Para {
            “I—I’m a little girl,” said Alice, rather doubtfully, as she
            remembered the number of changes she had gone through that day.
        }

        \Para {
            “A likely story indeed!” said the Pigeon in a tone of the deepest
            contempt. “I’ve seen a good many little girls in my time, but never
            \Italic{one} with such a neck as that! No, no! You’re a serpent; and
            there’s no use denying it. I sup\-pose you’ll be telling me next that
            you never tasted an egg!”
        }

        \Para {
            “I \Italic{have} tasted eggs, certainly,” said Alice, who was a very
            truthful child; “but little girls eat eggs quite as much as serpents
            do, you know.”
        }

        \Para {
            “I don’t believe it,” said the Pigeon; “but if they do, why then
            they’re a kind of serpent, that’s all I can say.”
        }

        \Para {
            This was such a new idea to Alice, that she was quite silent for a
            minute or two, which gave the Pigeon the opportunity of adding,
            “You’re looking for eggs, I know \Italic{that} well enough; and what
            does it matter to me whether you’re a little girl or a serpent?”
        }

        \Para {
            “It matters a good deal to \Italic{me,}” said Alice hastily; “but
            I’m not looking for eggs, as it happens; and if I was, I shouldn’t
            want \Italic{yours:} I don’t like them raw.”
        }

        \Para {
            “Well, be off, then!” said the Pigeon in a sulky tone, as it settled
            down again into its nest. Alice crouched down among the trees as
            well as she could, for her neck kept getting entangled among the
            branches, and every now and then she had to stop and untwist it.
            After a while she remembered that she still held the pieces of
            mushroom in her hands, and she set to work very carefully, nibbling
            first at one and then at the other, and growing some\-times taller and
            sometimes shorter, until she had succeeded in bringing herself down
            to her usual height.
        }

        \Para {
            It was so long since she had been anything near the right size, that
            it felt quite strange at first; but she got used to it in a few
            minutes, and began talking to herself, as usual. “Come, there’s half
            my plan done now! How puzzling all these changes are! I’m never sure
            what I’m going to be, from one minute to another! However, I’ve got
            back to my right size: the next thing is, to get into that beau\-tiful
            garden—how \Italic{is} that to be done, I won\-der?” As she said this,
            she came suddenly upon an open place, with a little house in it
            about four feet high. “Whoever lives there,” thought Alice, “it’ll
            never do to come upon them \Italic{this} size: why, I should
            frighten them out of their wits!” So she began nibbling at the
            right\=hand bit again, and did not venture to go near the house till
            she had brought herself down to nine inches high.
        }
    };

    \alias right: Distance = x + width;
    \alias height: Distance = ch.height;
    \alias bottom: Distance = y + height;
}
